 First, we find the probability that she does not solve it in the first five tries multiplied by the probability that she does solve it on the sixth try.  This is equivalent  to $\left({\dfrac{3}{4}}\right)^5\left(\dfrac{1}{4}\right)$.  The reason that we multiply these two probabilities is that these two events are entirely independent of each other, so multiplying these probabilities produces the total probability that this sequence of events occurs.  This is equivalent to $\dfrac{243}{1024}$.  We can think of all the cases in which she solves the problem before her sixth try as having the form [Thinking of the Problem<enter text>times] times [Solving the Problem<enter text>times], where the first string can be empty and the second string must be at least one character long.  By Stars and Bars we see that there are $\binom{5}{0} + \binom{5}{1} + \dotsb + \binom{5}{5} = 2^5 = 32$ subsets in this case, each of which can be arranged into a $2^5$ term polynomial.  Summing equal powers of $x$ across all degrees and setting $x = 1$ gives us a $2^5 = 32$ sum.  Since the constant term is the same whenever the sum is taken, we divide $2^6 = 64$ to get $\dfrac{64}{1024}$ so we have a total probability of $\dfrac{32}{1024} + \dfrac{1}{1024} = \dfrac{33}{1024}$.
